+++ b/docs/engineering/agent-adaptor-shadow.md
@@ -0,0 +1,111 @@
+# Experimental Codex SDK backend
+
+This opt-in pilot routes the real Multica daemon through `agent-adaptor/v2`.
+The native backend remains the default. It is a local integration experiment,
+not a replacement for Multica's other provider backends or a production rollout.
+
+Set `MULTICA_CODEX_BACKEND=agent-adaptor` for the daemon. Empty or `native` uses
+the existing implementation; unknown values fail configuration loading. Only
+Codex changes backend. Use separate native and shadow daemon profiles/runtimes
+for a comparison, with the same model, reasoning effort, prepared sandbox
+configuration, workspace permissions, and bounded run budget. This pilot uses
+Codex CLI 0.166.0, `gpt-6.1-sol`, and `xhigh` when explicitly selected by the host.
+
+The unreleased local SDK requires an external Go workspace. Do not commit an
+absolute local replacement or credentials into either repository. For example:
+
+```sh
+export GOWORK=/absolute/private/path/shadow.go.work
+go work init /absolute/path/multica/server /absolute/path/agent-adaptor
+go work edit -replace=github.com/agent-dance/agent-adaptor/v2@v2.0.0=/absolute/path/agent-adaptor
+```
+
+The `v2.0.0` requirement is the local experiment's workspace identity, not a
+claim that this revision has been published. Default tests use fake drivers
+and missing executables; they never discover the user's Codex installation or
+invoke a model. A real daemon run uses the existing external Codex profile and
+Multica's per-task profile materialization. Auth contents are never logged.
+
+## Actual route and receipts
+
+The normal UI/API/claim path remains in use: a comment or `@mention` creates or
+queues the host task, the daemon claims it, `ResolveBackend` chooses this
+backend, and the backend constructs a real public SDK Agent and business Thread.
+The existing server and daemon retain responsibility for comment routing,
+authorization, busy/pending decisions, task cancellation, and chat lifecycle.
+This integration does not replace those paths with a synthetic SDK-only test.
+
+For an active issue task, the daemon first waits for a real SDK `RunStarted`
+carrying an official provider turn ID and an inspected, version-gated message
+capability. A process launch or provider thread-ID announcement is insufficient.
+The durable supplement claim supplies its original comment ID, raw text, author
+kind, and display name to `Stream.Send` using `MessageID` and `From`. Older claim
+responses do not expose the actor's database ID; it is left empty rather than
+invented. SDK framing preserves human/peer authority.
+
+`Stream.Send` returning nil means admission. The typed supplement callback waits
+for actual `MessageDelivered` or `MessageFailed`, including cancellation races,
+before the existing HTTP claim/ACK flow reports delivered or a stable failure
+reason. The native string callback and its existing framing remain available.
+Chat keeps its existing cancel/new-task route; this pilot does not advertise
+mid-turn chat supplementation where the host did not negotiate it.
+
+SDK text, thinking, tool events, and results feed the existing daemon transcript
+and result paths. Only a healthy SDK checkpoint becomes the subsequent provider
+session handle; an initialization announcement cannot establish continuity.
+Cancel propagates through the run context. Result is published after the SDK
+event stream ends and `Agent.Close` confirms cleanup; unconfirmed cleanup is an
+explicit failure that prevents automatic continuation.
+
+## Continuity and evidence boundaries
+
+The business Thread key is a collision-free tuple of workspace, runtime, agent,
+issue/chat identity, or standalone-task identity. Checkpoints live in this
+daemon's memory and are shared across backend instances for that key. A missing
+provider handle never resets an existing key. A new host conversation needs a
+new business identity. Legacy or unknown provider handles are explicitly
+unsupported; this pilot does not import native provider sessions or prove
+recovery after killing/restarting the daemon.
+
+All task environment values remain in the real SDK configuration. Per-task
+tokens, task IDs, temporary directories, config roots, and `CODEX_HOME` commonly
+change. The SDK may consequently start a fresh provider conversation instead of
+resuming an incompatible checkpoint. Standard `Thread(key)` owns that one safe
+fallback, carries its bounded recent context, and emits `thread.continuity`.
+That actual notice becomes a visible `[Host continuity warning]` and a warning
+in the daemon log. It does not claim full-history recovery. The shadow prompt
+and runtime brief conservatively request cold-context behavior; the daemon's
+native preflight file heuristics and outer fresh-session retry are bypassed for
+shadow, leaving fallback ownership with the SDK. Healthy unchanged configuration
+still resumes the actual checkpoint. A failed replacement preserves the older
+healthy checkpoint and is not automatically replayed by the host.
+
+Keep the real daemon log alongside UI/API evidence. Structured records correlate
+`task_id`, actual SDK `run_id`, official live `turn_id`, comment `message_id`,
+receipt state/boundary, old/observed/healthy provider IDs, `fresh_fallback`, and
+the final `cleanup_confirmed` value. `MessageLog` alone is not persisted by
+Multica's task-message API. API cancellation timestamps are not evidence that a
+provider process exited: use the actual shadow execution-finished record before
+the next execution-started record. No token or raw auth/config file belongs in
+the report.
+
+## Remaining pilot limits
+
+- Custom launch prefixes and non-Codex providers are explicitly unsupported.
+- Native Codex's separate handshake, first-progress, semantic inactivity, thread
+  handshake, and interrupt timeout options are not mapped individually. Set an
+  explicit short total execution timeout and daemon idle budget for real tests;
+  an SDK execution still obeys its run context and bounded close.
+- The sandbox inherits the same daemon-prepared `CODEX_HOME`; permission approval
+  is SDK `AutoApprove`, matching the native daemon's automatic approval behavior.
+  This is not an additional sandbox. Verify the materialized sandbox mode and
+  actual workspace/network access for the native/shadow comparison.
+- No persistent process pool is shared across tasks: each configured Agent is
+  closed after its execution, so new task environment reaches a new process.
+- SDK Usage total input includes cached input. The adapter subtracts cache reads
+  to match Multica's exclusive accounting buckets; unobserved usage stays absent
+  and estimated SDK costs are not reported as billed provider cost.
+
+The new dependency is isolated to `pkg/agent` and this opt-in route. Using the
+reviewed SDK avoids duplicating its Thread, receipt, cancellation, and checkpoint
+contracts; the local unreleased revision remains an explicit rollout risk.
